  {
    "episode_id": 93205,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/3/93205.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 12,
    "turn_text": "later. And we still don't have a national plan. So because the president doesn't have a national plan, and because he's not using the Defense Production Act to get us the personal protection equipment and reagents and test kits we need, we're kind of on our own as a state. So I've spent a lot of my time trying to help the state get the supplies they need, trying to make sure that we're working in every possible way to get testing ramped up here. We have some very unique things here that I hope the governor is going to take advantage of and I understand he is. But you know, having exact sciences in Promega, you know, making reagents and doing test kits. It's a game changer. I just talked to a colleague, Peter Defazio from Oregon, 10 minutes ago, 15 minutes ago. And I mentioned that exact sciences right now can do 60,000 tests a week and they can ramp up to 120,000. And we're only doing two or 3,000 a day right now in Wisconsin. And we have to triple at least the number of tests to be able to safely reopen. In our own backyard, we have this tremendous resource that State of California is contracting with them for a few weeks right now for 20,000 kids. We really can get in the Midwest, if not a national example of how to do this right. If we start ordering tests in nursing homes and long-term care facilities, start making sure that everyone who works as a frontline worker in a hospital, clinic, police, and fire are all tested. Make sure that every clinic in the state, no matter what you come in for, are getting tested because that's what we have to start doing to know who has the virus and who doesn't, and then how to make sure that we're stopping the connections with people who are sick because a lot of people are asymptomatic who get this. I think most of my time these days is either fighting with the federal government trying to get supplies we need in Wisconsin or trying to take advantage of this resource we have here and figure out how we can reopen safely in our state and also working towards the next CARES package, the CARES 2 package, which will be in about two weeks to make sure we're picking up everyone who got left behind that we're continuing to do the mitigation that we need to, making sure that people who've lost a job through no fault of their own, or for the small business who has had no business through no fault of their own, that people are made whole so that we can get through this altogether.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
