    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "unemployment insurance. 27 states and DC have it. Wisconsin is one. And this is a bipartisan success because when they just did the bill in the legislature, Robin Voss and the governor were in the same place. They took every change that I recommended for the program. But what the program is is it allows you as a small business to ramp back up because you're not going to start back at 100% when the governor's safer at home order is done for most businesses. I can tell you, my own business, we're probably at 10% of what we're normally at right now, right? But if I can ramp up and start my employees right now, the legal limit, unless we do a federal law change, is it 40% of their hours and you put your workers at 40%, they can still then also access 60% of unemployment, including that surge money and have more money in their pocket so they'll be basically made whole, the small business can ramp up so they don't have to either keep you laid off or put you on it 100% which is a very tough decision because that means a lot of people are still going to be laid off and it takes away less from the UI account because the employer is paying someone to do the work that they have. So if I can, I'm trying to get the federal law to change it down to even 20% of work Because even any percent, you're getting your benefits, which is really important, your health insurance, because most people get it through their employer, not everyone, but most people in this country do. They can at least be working and helping that business. And as soon as business cranks up to 40 or 60 or 80%, you're going to do the exact same thing. But it keeps more money in the employee's pocket. It allows a small business to ramp up. And it saves the UI account. Win, win, win, but 23 states don't have it. And we got to make sure they have access to it. And also, we have to do that little tweak to lower the hours a little more. Right now, the state did lower the hours. They lowered it. It used to be 20 employees or more. Now, long as you have two employees, you can participate. And they're already into the thousands of people that are doing it. When one year ago, one business was in work share. So it's a success to help a business get ramped back up. But the states have to do this, not the",
